        "DEFINITION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "canonical_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Petroleum hydrocarbons are a diverse group of products derived or synthesized from crude oil.",
              "pdf_page": 1156,
              "printed_page": 1074,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Certain nonpetroleum-origin hydrocarbons, such as turpentine and linseed oil, are toxico logically similar enough to be considered with petroleum-origin products of similar molecular weight. Halogenated hydrocarbons, such as carbon tetrachloride or methylene chloride, are sufficiently unique to warrant separate consideration.",
              "pdf_page": 1156,
              "printed_page": 1074,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Small animal poisoning most commonly results from exposure to refined commercial products including such disparate mixtures as fuels, solvents, lubricants, and waxes.",
              "pdf_page": 1156,
              "printed_page": 1074,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Petroleum-based solvents are often used as “inert” carriers for other potential toxicants (e.g., pesticides, paints, medications).",
              "pdf_page": 1156,
              "printed_page": 1074,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Most petroleum hydrocarbons can be “lumped” into a relatively few broad categories based on volatility, viscosity, and surface tension. Mixtures with high boiling points (low volatility), such as asphalt, mineral oil, and waxes, are relatively nontoxic. Products with relatively low boiling points and low viscosities, such as benzene or turpentine, are more readily aspirated, penetrate further into airways, and are more likely to cause chemical pneumonitis. In general, products that are more volatile also tend to be more lipophilic and more readily absorbed systemically. Products with high aromatic content are also predisposed to cause systemic toxicity.",
              "pdf_page": 1156,
              "printed_page": 1074,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "In general, the mostacutelylife-threatening effects of hydrocarbon ingestion result from aspiration-induced pneumonitis.",
              "pdf_page": 1156,
              "printed_page": 1074,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Viscosity and surface tension are reliable determinants of pneumotoxic potential. Low viscosity permits hydrocarbons to penetrate further into smaller airways. Low surface tension increases their tendency to “wet” pulmonary surfaces. For example, aspiration of as little as 0.1 mL of a low-viscosity hydrocarbon (e.g., hexane) may produce severe pneumonitis, whereas a high-viscosity product (e.g., motor oil) would not penetrate past the major airways.",
              "pdf_page": 1156,
              "printed_page": 1074,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Inhalation of hydrocarbon vapors (as opposed to aspirating liquid) may compromise pulmonary immune function and displace oxygen.",
              "pdf_page": 1156,
              "printed_page": 1074,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Topical exposure to hydrocarbon-based solvents (e.g., petroleum distillates, turpentine) may result in irritation and necrosis of skin and cornea.",
              "pdf_page": 1156,
              "printed_page": 1074,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Systemic toxicity is possible after oral or topical exposure. Although there are no quantitative data readily applicable to small animals, systemic toxicity should be considered",
              "pdf_page": 1156,
              "printed_page": 1074,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "when evaluating pets that have received a ingesting hydrocarbons, evidenced by heavy topical exposure if the hydrocarbon is slobbering, champing the jaws, shaking the aromatic (e.g., benzene) or a low molecular head, and pawing at the muzzle. weight (e.g., hexane). Topical exposure is",
              "pdf_page": 1156,
              "printed_page": 1074,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Fever in 3–4 hours following aspiration; especially important in very small animals may occur in less than an hour or as much as (e.g., puppies, kittens, rodents), which have a 24 hours. relatively high body surface area to mass ratio.",
              "pdf_page": 1156,
              "printed_page": 1074,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Vomiting, colic, and diarrhea after oral Systemic uptake and thus toxicity are also exposure. The severity and presence of such enhanced by factors such as a long hair coat, signs are a function of the dose and the which traps the product against the skin. individual hydrocarbon. Heavy aliphatic hydrocarbons (e.g., mineral oil) may produce",
